SUMMARY:Palestine 36 w/ filmmaker | Arthouse Now
DESCRIPTION:In 1936\, as the British Empire tightens its grip on Palestin
 e\, Yusuf is caught between his village home and his work in Jerusalem. A
 midst an anti-colonial revolt and a workers uprising\, all sides converge
  in a decisive moment for the entire region. Weaving together multiple pl
 otlines\, empathies\, and frames of reference\, Annemarie Jacir deftly ba
 lances intimate human stories—portrayed by a stellar ensemble cast—wi
 th sweeping political history\, using rare archival footage to ground the
  narrative in historical reality. [119 min\; drama\; Arabic with English 
 subtitles and English]A prerecorded conversation with filmmaker Annemarie
  Jacir\, unique to IU Cinema\, will screen after the film. Jacir has writ
 ten\, directed\, and produced over 16 films. Her films have premiered in 
 Cannes\, Berlin\, Venice\, Locarno\, Rotterdam\, and Toronto. Her second 
 work to debut in Cannes\, Salt of This Sea\, went on to win the FIPRESCI 
 Critics Award\, and 14 other international awards including Best Film in 
 Milan. With a commitment to mentoring\, training\, and hiring locally\, J
 acir also teaches\, actively promoting independent cinema in the region. 
 Founder of Philistine Films\, she collaborates regularly as an editor\, s
 creenwriter\, and producer with fellow filmmakers.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  In 1936\, as the British Empire tighten
 s its grip on Palestine\, Yusuf is caught between his village home and hi
 s work in Jerusalem. Amidst an anti-colonial revolt and a workers uprisin
 g\, all sides converge in a decisive moment for the entire region. Weavin
 g together multiple plotlines\, empathies\, and frames of reference\, Ann
 emarie Jacir deftly balances intimate human stories—portrayed by a stel
 lar ensemble cast—with sweeping political history\, using rare archival
  footage to ground the narrative in historical reality. [119 min\; drama\
 ; Arabic with English subtitles and English]</p><p><i><strong>A prerecord
 ed conversation with filmmaker Annemarie Jacir\, unique to IU Cinema\, wi
 ll screen after the film. </strong></i>Jacir has written\, directed\, and
  produced over 16 films. Her films have premiered in Cannes\, Berlin\, Ve
 nice\, Locarno\, Rotterdam\, and Toronto. Her second work to debut in Can
 nes\, <i>Salt of This Sea\,</i> went on to win the FIPRESCI Critics Award
 \, and 14 other international awards including Best Film in Milan. With a
  commitment to mentoring\, training\, and hiring locally\, Jacir also tea
 ches\, actively promoting independent cinema in the region. Founder of Ph
 ilistine Films\, she collaborates regularly as an editor\, screenwriter\,
  and producer with fellow filmmakers.</p>
