  collapsed bg-none section" id="content"><div class="row"><div class="lay
 out"><div class="text"><p class="text screening-highlight"><strong>SNEAK 
 PREVIEW SCREENING! See Danny Boyle's acclaimed biopic of media magnate Ru
 pert Murdoch before it's available anywhere.</strong></p></div></div></di
 v></div><div class="section bg-none individual-film__intro"><div class="r
 ow"><div class="layout"><div class="text"><p>          In November 1969\,
  30-something outcast British journalist Larry Lamb (Jack O'Connell—<i>
 Sinners\; 28 Years Later</i>) met a young Australian media baron named Ru
 pert Murdoch (Guy Pearce—<i>Memento\; L.A. Confidential\; The Brutalist
 </i>) and a partnership began that changed the face of the news forever. 
 In the London of that era\, the media had its home on the iconic Fleet St
 reet\, a hotbed of betrayal\, backstabbing\, and bad behavior\, where it 
 was ruled by the establishment. Lamb and Murdoch took a different approac
 h\, publishing editions of <i>The Sun</i> loaded with lurid headlines and
  outlandish puns\, and covering everything from sports to television to s
 ex\, pushing ethical concerns to the sidelines\, and revolutionizing the 
 media landscape. This is the period that Academy Award-winning director D
 anny Boyle's (<i>Slumdog Millionaire\; 28 Days Later\; Trainspotting\; 28
  Years Later</i>) film—written by James Graham\, whose original stage p
 lay <i>INK</i> won awards on both sides of the Atlantic—covers\, one th
 at saw a succession of firsts that would alter the trajectory of today's 
 24/7\, clickbait news landscape and take Lamb and Murdoch down a dark pat
 h. [116 min\; drama\; English]        </p><p><i><strong>Special thanks to
  our friends at Netflix for providing this advance access screening</stro
 ng></i>.        </p></div></div></div></div>
